Actually, it's quite noble. Richard Feynman urges that we always try to make
an estimate of some quantity (a calculation, a physical magnitude,
etc.) before seeing the answer--this exercises our estimation skills,
gives us feedback on how to make better estimates in the future, and
increases our confidence when we make pretty good estimates.

Betting is a way to do this. Small bets about future outcomes, or
about something observable, are excellent ways to exercise this skill.

Heinlein has some of his characters--I think it was in "Moon is a
Harsh Mistress," notably--betting on nearly everything that comes
along. They bet on the weather the next day (just kidding..they were
on the moon), politics, etc.

I had an engineer at Intel that I must've won 50 Dr. Peppers off of
over a few years. Gary would make some claim, e.g., that Basic came
before Fortran, and I'd say "A Coke says you're wrong." When he
replied, "No, you're wrong...I _know_ Basic was invented first!" I'd
of course offer to double the stakes. One bet--not this one--got up to
*20* Cokes before we agreed to settle the bet. Needless to say, I won.
I did lose one or two bets, but that's all. (Eventually Gary stopped
betting with me. This meant he learned something valuable. Or trusted
what I said more. In any case, valuable.)

Recently, a friend of mine remarked that the actor in "Jurassic Park"
who played the fat hacker was the same actor who played the "Gopher,"
the fat kid in "Animal House." I said this wasn't so. He bet me a
fourpack of "Bartles and James" and I accepted. Later, he was so
convinced he offered to double the stakes. I played along, and the
stakes got doubled yet again, to four fourpacks. Then I told him that
while I didn't know the name of the actor in "JP"--though I suspect
it's Wolf as a surname--I did remember "Gopher" in "Animal House" was
played by Steven Furst, and he most certainly was not in "JP." My
friend's face fell when he heard this, because it rung true to him,
and he gamely agrees we can confirm the details by looking at the
proper books of movie reviews.

My point: bets add piquancy. And as the street puts it: "Money talks,
bullshit walks."

Several years back I was at a big science fiction party just days after
the Pons and Fleischman announcement. I ran a betting pool on these
issues:

- is cold fusion real?

- if it's real, how long before "Mr. Fusion"-type household
commercialization?

Nearly everyone at the party participated--including Eric Drexler,
Chip Morningstar (whose house the party was at), and others you're all
familiar with--and had a grand old time. It was really more of a
Delphi poll, and I didn't collect money. Somewhere I may still have
the sheet.

The point is, this exercise makes people carefully examine their
beliefs. I grant you it doesn't actually _prove_ anything (especially
in this kind of Delphi poll), but it does cause more careful estimates
to be made. And the economic incentive of a bet can cause the proof to
be found (as with my friend and I and the "Jurassic Park" actor: I'm
certainly motivated to look up the particulars next time I'm in a
bookstore).

If you ask the average American if he believes in ESP, he'll say he
does. If, however, you put the question in the form of a bet, about
some type of verification or convincing demonstration, his real common
sense--paradoxically--takes over and he'll likely side with the
skeptics.

Read Robin Hanson's article on idea futures and betting markets in a
past issue of "Extropy" (maybe #8 or #9).

Bets are great ways to discover truth. No philosophical mumbo jumbo,
just economic reality.

I may start using the betting system on this list more often. The
trouble is that I have much less patience in some disputes than even
Perry has, and simply skip boring threads.

But I'll keep my eyes open for some quick buck bets.


-Tim

