Tim May:
> - I don't know how many bets Perry has made, but my recollection is
> that bets have been offered several times since I joined the list last
> summer (July, '92). Never has anyone taken Perry up on these bets, to
> my knowledge. This says more about the others than it does about
> Perry, as the point above makes. 

Minor addendum: I was the recipient of one of Perry's offers.  I
counter-offered with a bet that more accurately reflected my claim.
Neither of us accepted each other's version of the bet.

In general I greatly encourage Perry and others to lay wagers, but the 
stakes and transaction costs have to be reasonable (Thornes would be 
good), the issue must be falsifiable in the short term, the bet has to 
be well-defined, and it should accurately represent the claims of both 
parties. 

Defining the issue well enough to create a falsifiable, mutually
agreeable wager is actually a good exercise for sharpening one's thinking 
about the issue, even if the wagers aren't actually made.

Short-term falsification is quite important. Betting on issues like 
"patient A will be reanimated before patient B", for example, is for 
now a fruitless exercise.

> Several years back I was at a big science fiction party just days after
> the Pons and Fleischman announcement. I ran a betting pool on these
> issues:
> 
> - is cold fusion real?

People disagree to this day.  Can't resolve the wager.
Also ambiguities: for example what if the process produces net power 
but it turns out it's not really "fusion"?   

> - if it's real, how long before "Mr. Fusion"-type household
> commercialization?

Unless one is quite optimistic, this is too long-term an issue.
Also, the optimists can always hold out hope, so the pessimists
can never collect.  (We could work out more sophisticated schemes,
for example the optimists pay out $X per year and the pessimists
pay out lump sum $Y when we get household fusion, but the transaction
costs quickly get prohibitive). 

The questions as stated are probably too ill-defined for wagers.

We currently have to sets of "idea futures" wagers going on Hex:
lifespan (80,100,1000,1e6, and 1e9 years), and long-term SSTO
launch costs (DCFLOP, etc.)  These are I think fairly
well defined; unfortuneately they aren't true "idea futures" in
that they track only what investors want them to track, they
don't actually pay off upon falsification.   (It'd be great
if Rowan or somebody else sets up a system for making payoffs).  
Also, they don't fit the short-term criterion very well.
They are more intended to track current opinion about long-term
issues; presumably opinion converges on reality as time goes
on.

> I may start using the betting system on this list more often. 

Not counting my previous bets, I'll bet you p1,000 that I make a bet on
this list before you do.  (I'll accept dollars:Thornes for $1:p,1000, 
but only for wagers, so this wager can be met with either $1
or p1,000).

